# Sync to External Platforms

HD is the source format. To deliver documentation to readers you publish it to an external platform — Nextra, Docusaurus, GitBook, and so on. The `hd` Claude plugin handles this with a single slash command, `/hd:sync`, driven by a small per-directory config file.

## The model

-   **One source directory** — the folder of `.hd` files you want to publish.
    
-   **One config file** — `hd-sync.json`, placed inside that directory. It defines what counts as part of the export and where it goes.
    
-   **One or more destinations** — each destination is a publishing target with its own platform type (e.g., Nextra 4) and its own output location. A single source can publish to many destinations at once.
    
-   **The slash command** — `/hd:sync` discovers configs in the codebase, lets you pick a destination, and runs the conversion.
    

Sync is one-way: HD → target. Files in the target are generated artifacts and will be overwritten on the next run.

## Setting up a directory to sync

### 1\. Create `hd-sync.json` in the source directory

Place the file alongside the `.hd` files it controls. The config holds an array of destinations:

```json
{
  "destinations": [
    {
      "name": "nextra 4",
      "type": "nextra4",
      "description": "Public docs site.",
      "path": "../../Docs/hd-docs",
      "contentDir": "src/content",
      "publicDir": "public",
      "assetMap": [
        { "from": "images", "to": "public/images" }
      ],
      "meta": {
        "index": { "title": "Overview", "theme": { "breadcrumb": false } },
        "getting-started": "Getting Started"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### 2\. Common destination fields

| Field | Required | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | yes | Unique label within this config. Shown in the destination picker. |
| `type` | yes | Converter key. Currently supported: `nextra4`. |
| `path` | yes | Path to the target project root. Resolved relative to the config file. |
| `description` | no | One-line description shown in the destination picker. |
| `routePrefix` | no | String prepended to internal link URLs and to the asset folder path. Required when multiple HD projects publish into the same target repo. See [Sharing a target repo](#multi-project). |
| `deleteOrphans` | no | `true` \| `false` \| `"warn"` (default `"warn"`). Controls whether stale files in the target are removed, warned about, or ignored. See [Orphan handling](#orphans). |

Additional fields are owned by each destination type. The `nextra4` type accepts `contentDir`, `publicDir`, `assetMap`, and `meta` — see the `hd-to-mdx` skill for the full reference.

### 3\. Multiple destinations

To publish the same source to several platforms, add more entries to the `destinations` array:

```json
{
  "destinations": [
    { "name": "nextra 4", "type": "nextra4", "path": "../../Docs/hd-nextra", "...": "..." },
    { "name": "docusaurus", "type": "docusaurus", "path": "../../Docs/hd-docusaurus", "...": "..." },
    { "name": "gitbook", "type": "gitbook", "path": "../../Docs/hd-gitbook", "...": "..." }
  ]
}
```

Each destination is independent. Running the sync against one does not affect the others.

## Running a sync

### The slash command

From any working directory at or above the source directory, run:

```
/hd:sync
```

The command walks the working directory looking for `hd-sync.json` files (up to four levels deep, skipping `node_modules`, `dist`, `.git`, and similar). What happens next depends on what it finds:

| Discovery result | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| No configs | Reports that none were found and exits. |
| One config, one destination | Runs without prompting. |
| One config, multiple destinations | Asks which destination. |
| Multiple configs | Asks which config, then which destination. |

### Direct CLI

The plugin's underlying script is also runnable directly, which is useful for scripting or for CI:

```
node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs                                  # list discovered configs
node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs --list                           # same, JSON
node plugin/hd/scripts/convert.mjs --config <path> --dest "<name>"  # run a sync
```

When using `--config` without `--dest`, the script will run the sync automatically only if the config has exactly one destination; otherwise it lists what's available and exits.

## Orphan handling

An *orphan* is a file in the target with no corresponding source on this sync — usually because the HD file was renamed or deleted but the previous output is still sitting in the target.

The `deleteOrphans` field controls what the sync does with them:

| Value | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `"warn"` (default) | Lists orphans in the sync output as warnings. Leaves them in place. |
| `true` | Deletes orphan `.mdx` files from `contentDir`. If `routePrefix` is set, also deletes orphan asset folders from `publicDir/assets/<routePrefix>/`. |
| `false` | Silent — no warning, no deletion. |

**Safety property:** deletion is always scoped to this project's own folders. The `.mdx` sweep only reads `contentDir`, which each project owns. The asset sweep only reads `publicDir/assets/<routePrefix>/`, which is unique per project. A sync cannot see, let alone delete, files belonging to another project — even when they share the same target repo.

If `deleteOrphans: true` is set without `routePrefix`, asset cleanup is skipped (with a warning explaining why), because `publicDir/assets/` would be a shared namespace. `.mdx` deletion still runs because `contentDir` is project-scoped either way.

## Sharing a target repo across projects

Multiple HD source directories can publish into the same Nextra target repo — for example, a product documentation site that aggregates docs from several internal codebases. To stay isolated, each project must:

1.  Use its own `contentDir` sub-folder (e.g., `src/content/projA`, `src/content/projB`).
    
2.  Set `routePrefix` to match the sub-folder (e.g., `"projA"`).
    

With both set, link and asset URLs are rewritten with the prefix, asset files are written to a per-project sub-folder under `publicDir/assets/<routePrefix>/`, and orphan deletion is bounded to that sub-folder. Two configs with different prefixes are physically incapable of touching each other's output.

```json
{
  "destinations": [
    {
      "name": "nextra 4",
      "type": "nextra4",
      "path": "../../Docs/hd-docs",
      "contentDir": "src/content/projA",
      "routePrefix": "projA",
      "deleteOrphans": true
    }
  ]
}
```

Without `routePrefix`, internal `.hd` links rewrite to `/foo` (the repo root), which is correct only when the project's `contentDir` is `src/content` itself. If `contentDir` is a sub-folder and `routePrefix` is missing, internal links between docs in the same project will 404.

## What the sync does and doesn't do

### Does

-   Converts every `.hd` file in the source directory to the destination format.
    
-   Rewrites internal `.hd` links to the platform's route style (with `routePrefix` applied if set).
    
-   Copies assets per `assetMap` and per-doc asset folders.
    
-   Generates the platform's sidebar or manifest file from the config (e.g., `_meta.js` for Nextra).
    
-   Optionally deletes orphan output when `deleteOrphans: true` is set (see [Orphan handling](#orphans)).
    
-   Reports counts and warnings.
    

### Doesn't

-   **Doesn't sync in reverse.** Hand-edits in the target will not survive the next sync. If you need platform-specific customization, do it in the source HD file or upstream into the converter.
    
-   **Doesn't delete files in the target unless asked.** The default `deleteOrphans: "warn"` only flags orphans. Set `true` to actually remove them.
    
-   **Doesn't look outside the source directory.** Links in `.hd` files to files outside the source dir (for example, `../README.md` or another HD file at the project root) are flagged as warnings and left unchanged. They will resolve as broken links in the published site.
    

## Warnings you may see

| Warning | What it means | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `external .hd reference (outside source dir)` | A link to a `.hd` file above the source directory. | Move the file into the source dir, drop the link, or rewrite to an absolute URL. |
| `external repo link not rewritten` | A link to a non-`.hd` file above the source directory (e.g., a `.md` at the project root). | Same options as above. |
| `asset source missing` | An `assetMap` entry pointed at a directory that doesn't exist. | Fix the path in `hd-sync.json` or remove the entry. |
| `orphan in target (no HD source)` | A `.mdx` in the target's content directory has no corresponding `.hd` source. | Delete manually, or set `deleteOrphans: true` on the destination to automate it. |
| `orphan asset folder (no HD source)` | An asset folder under `publicDir/assets/<routePrefix>/` has no corresponding `.hd` source. Only emitted when `routePrefix` is set. | Delete manually, or set `deleteOrphans: true`. |
| `asset orphan cleanup skipped: no routePrefix set` | You set `deleteOrphans: true` but no `routePrefix`. Asset deletion was skipped to avoid accidentally deleting another project's files under a shared `publicDir/assets/`. | Add `routePrefix`, or accept that assets won't be auto-cleaned. |

## After a sync

The sync produces source files for the external platform; it does not build the site itself. Run the platform's build to verify the output compiles and the pages render. For Nextra 4 that's `npm run build` in the target project — prerender is the only catch-all for MDX/JSX errors the converter can't predict.

